When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n---\nname: scientific-evidence\ndescription: |\n  Use when: you start writing a scientific paper.\n  Outputs: a repo-local `scientific-evidence/` bundle generated by the skill script, plus the conventions, schemas, and verification report needed for downstream evidence tracking and auditing.\n---\n\n# scientific-evidence\n\nUse this skill to define or materialize a generic evidence contract for scientific artifacts.\n\nAlways use this skill during paper writing whenever the agent adds quantitative content: manuscript numbers, table entries, figure inputs, derived statistics, or plotting/calculation scripts.\n\n## What this skill is for\n\nThe point of this skill is simple: an agent should not be able to drop important numbers, figures, or derived outputs into a paper without leaving a machine-checkable trail back to real artifacts.\n\nThis skill gives the agent one fixed place and one fixed grammar for that trail:\n- inline `@evidence[[...]]` refs in text-like files\n- `.prov.json` sidecars for generated artifacts\n- a central `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl` for optional structured rollups\n\nIt is deliberately generic. The base contract works even when the workflow spans Python, bash, LaTeX, Markdown, JSON, logs, or other file formats.\n\n## Default workflow\n\n1. Read the current paper/results layout, but do not invent a new evidence layout.\n2. Use the fixed directory structure rooted at `scientific-evidence/`.\n3. Run the skill script first instead of inventing schemas by hand:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n4. For every quantitative paper-writing action, use this skill contract:\n- adding a number to `paper.tex`\n- filling a table cell\n- writing a plotting script\n- writing a calculation/statistics script\n- generating a figure, table, or derived JSON file\n5. Inside plotting or calculation scripts, do not hardcode experiment numbers that should come from artifacts. Read them from files, then leave the corresponding `@evidence[[...]]` refs near the read/derivation logic.\n6. Add inline evidence refs in text-like artifacts and `.prov.json` sidecars for generated outputs.\n7. Run the verifier after the agent writes claims, scripts, figures, or sidecars:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n8. Inspect `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` and fail hard on dangling links.\n9. Treat the generated markdown spec and JSON Schemas as canonical for downstream agents.\nKeep the verifier behavior explicit:\n- `resolved`: target artifact exists and the verifier can check the locator semantics it understands.\n- `anchored`: target artifact exists, but the locator stays opaque or only hash/path checks were possible.\n- `dangling`: the target or manifest link does not resolve.\n\n## How this is used to verify agent work\n\nVerification works by checking whether the agent left a connected evidence trail.\n\nAt a minimum, a downstream verifier should be able to:\n- scan manuscript text, scripts, and notes for `@evidence[[...]]` refs\n- read `.prov.json` sidecars for figures, tables, and other generated outputs\n- resolve those refs against real files or stable ids\n- classify each link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`\n- fail when a generated artifact has no input trail or when a claimed source does not exist\n\nThis does not prove every scientific claim automatically. It does prove whether the agent left enough structure for an auditor or stricter verifier to replay and inspect the work instead of trusting free-form prose.\n\nA core policy for paper writing is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should be read from real artifacts. Read from files, derive from those inputs, and attach `@evidence[[...]]` refs that point back to the inputs.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\n## Grammar summary\n\n- Evidence ref token: `@evidence[[TARGET]]` or `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `TARGET` may be a relative path, absolute path, URI, or stable identifier.\n- `LOCATOR` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Generated artifact sidecar name: `<artifact>.prov.json`\n- Central ledger file: `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Schema set\n\nGenerate the canonical schema bundle with the script. The bundle includes:\n- `scientific-evidence/spec.md`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Examples\n\nPython:\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\nBash:\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\nLaTeX:\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\nMarkdown:\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\nR:\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\nYAML:\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\nJSON sidecar:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nSmoke test the script, bundle generation, and verifier:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\n```\n{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        6\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"score_prediction_overview\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 9,\n        \"legend\": 9,\n        \"ticks\": 9,\n        \"title\": 10\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 30,\n      \"n_panels\": 1,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/score_prediction_overview.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"prompt_log_path\": \"figures/score_prediction_overview.prompts.json\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        1\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        4\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"paired_error_comparison\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"scatter\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 9,\n        \"legend\": 9,\n        \"ticks\": 9,\n        \"title\": 10\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 24,\n      \"n_panels\": 1,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/paired_error_comparison.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/paired_error_comparison.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        1\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        5,\n        4\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"case_budget_reversals\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"composite\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 9,\n        \"legend\": 9,\n        \"ticks\": 9,\n        \"title\": 10\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 24,\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/case_budget_reversals.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/case_budget_reversals.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"budget_sensitivity\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"line\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 9,\n        \"legend\": 9,\n        \"ticks\": 9,\n        \"title\": 10\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 24,\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/budget_sensitivity.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/budget_sensitivity.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        4,\n        4\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    }\n  ]\n}\npaper_statistics.json\npaper_statistics.json.prov.json\nrecorded_cell_metrics.csv\nrecorded_cell_metrics.csv.prov.json\n__pycache__\ncompile_latex.sh\nvalidate_compile_inputs.py\nvalidate_figures.py\nvalidate_rendered_si.py\nvalidate_tex_refs.py\nscientific_evidence.py\nscientific_evidence.sh\nappend_memory.py\npaper.tex:214:The HELM MATH contrast is substantial enough to matter to the interpretation: at 5 items the package's MAE is 11.63 percentage points, compared with 9.53 for IRT; at 25 items the respective values are 6.18 and 5.61. MedQA is also nonmonotonic for the package: its MAE increases from 6.68 at 5 items to 7.47 at 10, before falling to 5.54 at 25. Additional observations therefore do not ensure lower realized error under a changed regression prefix. We do not diagnose these reversals as saturation, lineage shift, or decoder activation: the retained evidence does not isolate those causes.\nnode-compile-cache\nopencode\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"ls scripts; sed -n '1,220p' scripts/build_paper_artifacts.py; sed -n '1,150p' paper_strategy.md; cat paper_checks.json; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py | head -100; sed -n '1,160p' .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py; sed -n '1,120p' REPRODUCE.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"ls scripts; sed -n '1,220p' scripts/build_paper_artifacts.py; sed -n '1,150p' paper_strategy.md; cat paper_checks.json; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py | head -100; sed -n '1,160p' .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py; sed -n '1,120p' REPRODUCE.md\"","aggregated_output":"__pycache__\nbuild_claim_mapping.py\nbuild_paper_artifacts.py\ncompile_local.sh\nretrieve_focused_references.py\nvalidate_local.py\n\"\"\"Recompute tables/plots from recorded predictions, without model execution.\n\n@evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n@evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#L545]]\n\"\"\"\n\nimport csv\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport matplotlib\nmatplotlib.use('Agg')\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.stats import kendalltau, spearmanr\n\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]\nMETHODS = ['selected', 'irt_mae', 'uniform_mae', 'prior_mae', 'anchor_mae']\nLABELS = {'selected': 'Score-targeted package', 'irt_mae': 'Local IRT',\n          'uniform_mae': 'Uniform', 'prior_mae': 'Source prior', 'anchor_mae': 'Local anchors'}\nCOLORS = {'selected': '#244b87', 'irt_mae': '#168077', 'uniform_mae': '#707782',\n          'prior_mae': '#875b9e', 'anchor_mae': '#c87728'}\nDISPLAY = {'bbh': 'BBH', 'gpqa': 'GPQA', 'mmlu_pro': 'MMLU-Pro', 'musr': 'MuSR',\n           'openllm_math': 'Leaderboard MATH', 'legalbench': 'LegalBench',\n           'math': 'HELM MATH', 'med_qa': 'MedQA', 'mmlu': 'MMLU'}\n\n\ndef write_json(path, value):\n    path.write_text(json.dumps(value, indent=2) + '\\n')\n\n\ndef provenance(path, sources):\n    write_json(Path(str(path) + '.prov.json'), {\n        'schema_name': 'scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest',\n        'artifact': {'path': str(path.relative_to(ROOT)), 'kind': 'derived',\n                     'sha256': hashlib.sha256(path.read_bytes()).hexdigest()},\n        'derived_from': sources,\n        'generated_by': {'script_path': 'scripts/build_paper_artifacts.py',\n                         'command': ['python', 'scripts/build_paper_artifacts.py'], 'cwd': '.'}})\n\n\ndef summarize(values):\n    return {metric: float(np.mean([value[metric] for value in values]))\n            for metric in ['mae', 'rmse', 'spearman', 'kendall']}\n\n\ndef metrics(prediction, truth, groups):\n    unique, inverse, counts = np.unique(groups, return_inverse=True, return_counts=True)\n    weights = 1.0 / (len(unique) * counts[inverse])\n    difference = prediction - truth\n    group_predictions = [prediction[groups == group].mean() for group in unique]\n    group_truth = [truth[groups == group].mean() for group in unique]\n    if len(set(group_predictions)) < 2 or len(set(group_truth)) < 2:\n        spearman, kendall = -1.0, -1.0\n    else:\n        spearman = spearmanr(group_predictions, group_truth).statistic\n        kendall = kendalltau(group_predictions, group_truth).statistic\n    return dict(mae=float(weights @ np.abs(difference)),\n                rmse=float(np.sqrt(weights @ difference ** 2)),\n                spearman=float(spearman), kendall=float(kendall))\n\n\ndef save_figure(figure, identifier):\n    sources = ['@evidence[[analysis/paper_statistics.json]]', '@evidence[[scripts/build_paper_artifacts.py]]']\n    for suffix in ['pdf', 'png']:\n        path = ROOT / 'figures' / (identifier + '.' + suffix)\n        figure.savefig(path, dpi=300, facecolor='white')\n        provenance(path, sources)\n    plt.close(figure)\n\n\ndef figure_entry(identifier, figure_type, panels, categories, series):\n    return dict(figure_id=identifier, figure_type=figure_type,\n                path_png='figures/' + identifier + '.png', path_pdf='figures/' + identifier + '.pdf',\n                generator='matplotlib', n_panels=panels, panels_per_row=1,\n                categories_per_panel=categories, series_per_panel=series,\n                max_label_chars=24, font_sizes=dict(title=10, axis=9, ticks=9, legend=9),\n                uses_abbrev_labels=True, uses_progression=False,\n                placement='full_width', intended_latex_width_fraction=1.0,\n                estimated_min_rendered_font_pt=9.0, source_ids=['final.evidence.episodes'])\n\n\ndef main():\n    paper = json.loads((ROOT / 'paper_data.json').read_text())\n    final = paper['final_evaluation']\n    outer_records = final['evaluation_evidence']['episodes']\n    uncertainty = outer_records[0]['solution']['paired_mae_uncertainty']\n    assert all(record['solution']['paired_mae_uncertainty'] == uncertainty for record in outer_records)\n    rows = []\n    keys = set()\n    for outer in outer_records:\n        for episode in outer['solution']['episodes']:\n            if episode['condition'] != 'normal' or episode.get('replay', False):\n                continue\n            for checkpoint in episode['checkpoints']:\n                key = (outer['seed'], episode['case'], checkpoint['budget'])\n                assert key not in keys\n                keys.add(key)\n                truth = np.array(checkpoint['references'])\n                groups = np.array(checkpoint['lineage_groups'])\n                for method in METHODS:\n                    prediction = np.array(checkpoint['predictions'] if method == 'selected'\n                                          else checkpoint['baseline_predictions'][method])\n                    rows.append(dict(seed=outer['seed'], case=episode['case'], budget=checkpoint['budget'],\n                                     method=method, target_rows=len(truth), target_groups=len(np.unique(groups)),\n                                     **metrics(prediction, truth, groups)))\n    aggregate = {method: summarize([row for row in rows if row['method'] == method]) for method in METHODS}\n    for method in METHODS:\n        metric_name = 'mae' if method == 'selected' else method\n        assert np.isclose(aggregate[method]['mae'], final['metrics'][metric_name], rtol=0, atol=1e-12)\n    for metric in ['rmse', 'spearman', 'kendall']:\n        assert np.isclose(aggregate['selected'][metric], final['metrics'][metric], rtol=0, atol=1e-12)\n    cases = list(dict.fromkeys(row['case'] for row in rows))\n    budgets = sorted(set(row['budget'] for row in rows))\n    seeds = sorted(set(row['seed'] for row in rows))\n    by_budget = {str(budget): {method: summarize([row for row in rows if row['budget'] == budget and row['method'] == method]) for method in METHODS} for budget in budgets}\n    by_case_budget = {case: {str(budget): {method: summarize([row for row in rows if row['case'] == case and row['budget'] == budget and row['method'] == method]) for method in METHODS} for budget in budgets} for case in cases}\n    by_seed = {str(seed): {method: summarize([row for row in rows if row['seed'] == seed and row['method'] == method]) for method in METHODS} for seed in seeds}\n    reversals = [(case, budget) for case in cases for budget in budgets\n                 if by_case_budget[case][str(budget)]['selected']['mae'] > by_case_budget[case][str(budget)]['irt_mae']['mae']]\n    summary = dict(aggregate=aggregate, by_budget=by_budget, by_case_budget=by_case_budget,\n                   by_seed=by_seed, cases=cases, seeds=seeds, budgets=budgets,\n                   cell_count=len(keys), uncertainty=uncertainty, local_irt_reversals=reversals,\n                   qualification={name: final[name] for name in ['status', 'outcome']},\n                   relative_mae_reduction_vs_irt=1-aggregate['selected']['mae']/aggregate['irt_mae']['mae'])\n    write_json(ROOT / 'analysis/paper_statistics.json', summary)\n    provenance(ROOT / 'analysis/paper_statistics.json', ['@evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation]]'])\n    with (ROOT / 'analysis/recorded_cell_metrics.csv').open('w') as stream:\n        writer = csv.DictWriter(stream, fieldnames=list(rows[0]))\n        writer.writeheader()\n        writer.writerows(rows)\n    provenance(ROOT / 'analysis/recorded_cell_metrics.csv', ['@evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]'])\n    main_rows = []\n    for method in METHODS:\n        values = aggregate[method]\n        cost = '0' if method == 'prior_mae' else '$k$'\n        ranking = '-- & --' if method == 'prior_mae' else f\"{values['spearman']:.3f} & {values['kendall']:.3f}\"\n        main_rows.append(f\"{LABELS[method]} & {cost} & {values['mae']*100:.2f} & {values['rmse']*100:.2f} & {ranking} \\\\\")\n    (ROOT / 'tables/aggregate.tex').write_text('% @evidence[[analysis/paper_statistics.json#/aggregate]]\\n' +\n        '\\\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\\n\\\\toprule\\nEstimator & Answers & MAE (pp) & RMSE (pp) & Spearman & Kendall\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\midrule\\n' +\n        '\\n'.join(row + '\\\\' for row in main_rows) + '\\n\\\\bottomrule\\n\\\\end{tabular}\\n')\n    case_rows = []\n    for case in cases:\n        for budget in budgets:\n            values = by_case_budget[case][str(budget)]\n            numeric = ' & '.join(f\"{values[method]['mae']*100:.2f}\" for method in METHODS)\n            case_rows.append(f\"{DISPLAY[case]} & {budget} & {numeric} \\\\\\\\\")\n    (ROOT / 'tables/case_budget.tex').write_text('% @evidence[[analysis/paper_statistics.json#/by_case_budget]]\\n' +\n        '\\\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}\\n\\\\toprule\\nBenchmark & Items & Package & IRT & Uniform & Prior & Anchors\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\midrule\\n' +\n        '\\n'.join(case_rows) + '\\n\\\\bottomrule\\n\\\\end{tabular}\\n')\n    seed_rows = []\n    for seed in seeds:\n        values = by_seed[str(seed)]['selected']\n        seed_rows.append(f\"{seed} & {values['mae']*100:.3f} & {values['rmse']*100:.3f} & {values['spearman']:.4f} & {values['kendall']:.4f} \\\\\\\\\")\n    (ROOT / 'tables/seeds.tex').write_text('% @evidence[[analysis/paper_statistics.json#/by_seed]]\\n' +\n        '\\\\begin{tabular}{rrrrr}\\n\\\\toprule\\nSeed & MAE & RMSE & Spearman & Kendall\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\midrule\\n' +\n        '\\n'.join(seed_rows) + '\\n\\\\bottomrule\\n\\\\end{tabular}\\n')\n    for path in (ROOT / 'tables').glob('*.tex'):\n        provenance(path, ['@evidence[[analysis/paper_statistics.json]]'])\n    plt.rcParams.update({'font.size': 9, 'axes.labelsize': 9, 'xtick.labelsize': 9,\n                         'ytick.labelsize': 9, 'legend.fontsize': 9, 'axes.spines.top': False,\n                         'axes.spines.right': False, 'pdf.fonttype': 42})\n    figure, axis = plt.subplots(figsize=(5.5, 2.2), layout='constrained')\n    comparators = ['irt_mae', 'prior_mae', 'uniform_mae', 'anchor_mae']\n    for position, method in enumerate(comparators):\n        comparison = uncertainty['comparisons'][method]\n        point, lower, upper = [comparison[field]*100 for field in ['candidate_minus_baseline_mae', 'lower_95', 'upper_95']]\n        axis.errorbar(point, position, xerr=[[point-lower], [upper-point]], fmt='o',\n                      capsize=4, color=COLORS[method], linewidth=1.8, markersize=5)\n    axis.axvline(0, color='#303030', linewidth=0.8)\n    axis.set(yticks=range(len(comparators)), yticklabels=[LABELS[method] for method in comparators],\n             xlabel='Package − comparator MAE (percentage points)', ylim=(-0.6, 3.6))\n    axis.invert_yaxis()\n    axis.grid(axis='x', alpha=0.15)\n    save_figure(figure, 'paired_error_comparison')\n    figure, axes = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(5.5, 3.65), sharex=True, layout='constrained',\n                                gridspec_kw={'height_ratios': [5, 4]})\n    ecosystem_cases = [cases[:5], cases[5:]]\n    markers = ['o', 's', '^']\n    for axis, subset, title in zip(axes, ecosystem_cases, ['(a) Leaderboard matrices', '(b) HELM matrices']):\n        for budget_index, budget in enumerate(budgets):\n            differences = [(by_case_budget[case][str(budget)]['selected']['mae'] - by_case_budget[case][str(budget)]['irt_mae']['mae'])*100 for case in subset]\n            axis.scatter(differences, np.arange(len(subset)) + (budget_index-1)*0.20,\n                         marker=markers[budget_index], color=COLORS['selected'], s=27, label=str(budget) + ' items')\n        axis.axvline(0, color='#333333', linewidth=0.8)\n        axis.set(yticks=range(len(subset)), yticklabels=[DISPLAY[case] for case in subset],\n                 ylim=(-0.55, len(subset)-0.45))\n        axis.invert_yaxis()\n        axis.set_title(title, loc='left', fontsize=10)\n        axis.grid(axis='x', alpha=0.15)\n    axes[0].legend(loc='lower left', ncol=3, frameon=False, bbox_to_anchor=(-0.02, 1.13), borderaxespad=0, handletextpad=0.3, columnspacing=0.9)\n    axes[1].set_xlabel('Package − local IRT MAE (percentage points)')\n    save_figure(figure, 'case_budget_reversals')\n    figure, axes = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(5.5, 3.8), sharex=True, layout='constrained')\n    for method in ['selected', 'irt_mae', 'uniform_mae', 'anchor_mae']:\n        axes[0].plot(budgets, [by_budget[str(budget)][method]['mae']*100 for budget in budgets],\n                     color=COLORS[method], marker='o', label=LABELS[method])\n        axes[1].plot(budgets, [by_budget[str(budget)][method]['spearman'] for budget in budgets],\n                     color=COLORS[method], marker='o')\n    axes[0].set_ylabel('MAE (percentage points)')\n    axes[1].set_ylabel('Spearman correlation')\n    axes[1].set_xlabel('Queried items per target')\n    axes[1].set_xticks(budgets)\n    axes[0].legend(ncol=2, fontsize=9, frameon=False)\n    for axis in axes:\n        axis.grid(alpha=0.15)\n    save_figure(figure, 'budget_sensitivity')\n    concept_png = ROOT / 'figures/score_prediction_overview.png'\n    concept_log = ROOT / 'figures/score_prediction_overview.prompts.json'\n    completed = (ROOT / '.concept_figure_complete').exists()\n    populated_log = concept_log.exists() and concept_log.read_text().strip() not in ['', '{}']\n    if not completed and not populated_log and not concept_png.exists():\n        figure = plt.figure(figsize=(8, 2.9))\n        figure.text(0.5, 0.5, 'Conceptual overview reserved for orchestrator rendering', ha='center', va='center', fontsize=15, color='#555555')\n        figure.savefig(concept_png, dpi=300, facecolor='#fafafa')\n        plt.close(figure)\n        concept_log.write_text('{}\\n')\n# Paper Framing Strategy\n\n**Status:** ready for a bounded standalone study; relevant-literature repair is required before final related-work writing.  \n**Prompt family:** `paper_standalone` / `write-paper-standalone`.  \n**Provisional title:** *Score-Targeted Subsets for Predicting LLM Benchmark Accuracy*.\n\n## Central Question and Thesis\n\n**Question.** How accurately can a source-trained, score-targeted shared item subset and decoder predict retained full-benchmark accuracy and model rankings at 5-, 10-, and 25-item budgets, relative to local benchmark-estimation baselines, and where does the comparison reverse?\n\n**Thesis.** On nine frozen benchmark matrices, the evaluated acquisition-and-decoding package has lower average accuracy-prediction error than the recorded baselines under equal benchmark, seed, and budget weighting. Its advantage over local IRT is not uniform across benchmarks. The measurements neither isolate an individual decoder component's benefit nor establish transfer beyond this snapshot.\n\nThis is one estimation question with condition-specific evidence from leaderboard and HELM matrices, not two chronological experiments. Lead with the estimand, information constraints, and paired comparison, then explain where the average is misleading. A lower average error is the measured result; universal superiority and nonlinear-mechanism attribution are not.\n\n## Independent Evidence and Qualification Audit\n\nRead the final record independently of any campaign narrative. `downstream_study` is absent. The authoritative qualification sources are:\n\n- `final.qualification.status`: `completed`.\n- `final.qualification.outcome`: `qualified`.\n- `final.qualification.correct`: `true`.\n\nThese facts do not establish complete ancestry separation, contamination absence, out-of-snapshot validity, or any unreported gate diagnosis. Publication readiness is not scientific qualification. If a later authoritative record reports failed qualification, preserve that failure and its reported limitations throughout writing, review, audit, and revision; never infer its cause from retained scores. Missing diagnostics remain unknown.\n\nThe final evidence contains three seeds, nine distinct cases, and budgets 5/10/25. A BBH normal replay is a determinism check, **not** an extra case or an independent measurement. The aggregate paired uncertainty object is repeated in each seed wrapper; use it once, not as three independent bootstrap results. Candidate-owned diagnostics are explicitly unverified.\n\n### Primary Numerical Anchors\n\n| Quantity | Recorded value | Grounding source |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| Selected package MAE | 0.038991605535293065 | `final.metric.mae` |\n| Selected package RMSE | 0.04837892636321264 | `final.metric.rmse` |\n| Local IRT MAE | 0.05911223215296765 | `final.metric.irt_mae` |\n| Uniform sampling MAE | 0.10520541810011626 | `final.metric.uniform_mae` |\n| Source prior MAE | 0.08335016635136305 | `final.metric.prior_mae` |\n| Response-vector anchor MAE | 0.15674229873896958 | `final.metric.anchor_mae` |\n| Selected package Spearman | 0.8158480130897703 | `final.metric.spearman` |\n| Selected package Kendall | 0.6715966634525837 | `final.metric.kendall` |\n\nAll errors use score units in `[0,1]`; these are not 25-item-only results. The evaluator averages the declared budgets, seeds, and cases, with target-lineage-equal errors within cells. Ranking is computed from lineage-mean predictions and truths within each cell, not by pooling every model from different benchmarks. An average of cell RMSEs must not silently become a pooled RMSE.\n\nThe recorded candidate-minus-IRT MAE is **-0.02012062661767458**, with recorded 95% interval **[-0.02570984836073398, -0.014632649205711504]**, grounded in `final.evidence.episodes`. Its 200-draw paired lineage-block bootstrap retains checkpoints and seeds within groups, averages cases equally, and conditions on the fixed cases. It is not uncertainty over new benchmarks, a component-ablation effect, or a generalization guarantee.\n\nThe raw predictions also support direct case/budget views. Diagnostic postprocessing excluding replay and applying the evaluator's lineage weights finds candidate losses to local IRT on **math at all three budgets**, **legalbench at 5 and 25**, and **med_qa at 10 and 25**. Keep those cells visible in the main paper. Regenerate exact plot values from the raw records rather than quoting rounded framing diagnostics. Do not invent causes for the reversals or reuse the aggregate interval as a per-case interval.\n\n## Framing Decision and Alternatives\n\n**Decision: recentered.** This workspace has no resolved edit-context file, previous manuscript, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` input. This is fresh framing; the label records a narrowing of the broad source-task brief, not an edit to an unseen paper. No inherited title or figure plan is authoritative.\n\nCompare the candidate framings explicitly:\n\n1. **Preserve a prior story with local updates:** no prior manuscript is supplied, and private memory is insufficient justification. Do not inherit a random-audit or discovery-order narrative.\n2. **Broaden to a cross-setting method family:** the two data ecosystems support a common estimation question, but not an adaptive-per-target acquisition claim or a general transfer mechanism. Use condition-specific evidence under one thesis, not an enlarged capability claim.\n3. **Recenter on the frozen final comparison:** strongest choice because it has paired baseline predictions, a defined scoring protocol, aggregate uncertainty, and scientifically informative reversals.\n\nReject a random-audit unbiasedness paper: that earlier design is not the final evaluated package. Reject a nonlinear-decoder superiority paper: the selected source tree includes an incumbent fallback, its per-case branch selection is not attested, and no controlled ablation bundle exists. Reject a search-progress paper: the central question concerns prediction quality, not optimization dynamics.\n\n## Implementation and Staged-Input Impact\n\nDirectly inspected local sources are under `results/ideas/03_log_odds_shift_residual_decoder/program_1/src/`: `program.py`, `surrogate.py`, and `nonlinear_decoder.py`, together with their implementation notes. The local selection manifest identifies this counterpart of the frozen selected package; no candidate was executed during framing.\n\nThe core mechanism is source-weighted covariance between each item response and the full source score. Greedy acquisition maximizes squared conditional score covariance divided by regularized conditional item variance, using a nested common path and excluding already represented content groups. All-query ridge/offset decoding uses every legal queried answer. The implementation also offers a source-selected penalized log-odds shift, original-population score curve, and prefix-matched residual ridge, retaining the linear fallback. Explain these as the implemented decoder menu, not as independently validated contributions.\n\n`task/evaluate_final.py`, frozen manifests, and `task/resources/rebuild.py` establish the evaluator definitions and processed data scope. They support metadata-defined source/target lineage separation and conservative item reconciliation, not proof against undocumented derivatives, semantic duplicates, or pretraining contamination. The truth to be predicted is the full mean over **retained matrix columns**, not necessarily every original benchmark item.\n\nNo existing figures or controlled ablations are available. Earlier implementations need not become main-paper narrative baggage. Their code may clarify design alternatives, but no final matched measurements support an original-program comparison. In particular, `task/program_0` is readable, but its old score from memory must not be imported. Keep the actual recorded comparators instead.\n\n## Narrow Method Set\n\n| Group | Role | Inclusion and boundary |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Score-targeted acquisition and source-selected score decoding | Proposed | Frozen final package; describe ridge/offset and optional nonlinear menu without asserting branch activation or component causality. |\n| Uniform content-group sampling | Baseline | Group-representative sampling with group-mass correction and clipping; not silently ordinary unweighted averaging. |\n| Source-score prior | Baseline | No target responses required; display observation cost as zero. |\n| Response-vector anchors | Baseline | Local normalized-response/farthest-first representative selection and cluster-weighted score estimate; not an exact published-method reproduction. |\n| Local Rasch-style IRT | Baseline | Strongest aggregate comparator; source-fitted item difficulty and queried target ability on the same uniform path as the sampling baseline. Not the full tinyBenchmarks system. |\n\nThe measured comparison changes both acquisition and decoding at matching query budgets. It does not isolate either component, guarantee equal training compute, or establish that every comparator received globally optimal tuning.\n\n## Results Organization\n\n1. **Aggregate accuracy and paired uncertainty.** A compact five-method table gives absolute MAE, observation cost, and separately labeled RMSE/ranking where supported. The forest plot shows the four supplied paired MAE differences and intervals. No performance-success threshold or internal pipeline metric is needed.\n2. **Benchmark-specific reversals.** Show all nine cases against IRT at each budget using signed differences with a common zero reference. Put the math, legalbench, and med_qa reversals in the main results and limitations, not only the supplement. Ecosystem panels are organizational, not evidence of a causal ecosystem effect.\n3. **Ranking as a distinct objective.** Report the selected package's recorded Spearman and Kendall. Baseline rankings may be derived reproducibly from existing predictions using the evaluator's lineage-mean definition; if not derived, mark them “not reported,” never zero or inferred. Do not claim ranking superiority from selected-package values alone.\n4. **Controls and evidence limits.** Permuted-source MAE is 0.06257521756337783 (`final.metric.permuted_mae`); zero-response-control MAE is 0.21577661527598374 (`final.metric.constant_mae`). The constant intervention zeroes target observations; it is not necessarily a constant predictor. The source intervention independently circular-shifts each source row's columns. Describe sensitivity, not isolated ablation or validity proof. Duplicate histories and deterministic replay are protocol checks, not accuracy improvements.\n\nDevelopment and final evaluations are separate. Development uses `arc_challenge`, `ifeval`, `commonsense`, and `gsm`, seeds 101/211. Final uses `bbh`, `gpqa`, `mmlu_pro`, `musr`, `openllm_math`, `legalbench`, `math`, `med_qa`, and `mmlu`, seeds 1009/2003/3001. Do not compare their absolute averages as a paired improvement. There are no `study.*` sources here: no search-score differences, campaign win rates, or scored/unscored performance accounting is proposed. An unscored attempt would be missing evidence, never a measured loss.\n\n## Figure Plan and Physical Layout\n\nThe inspected `paper.sty` sets **one 5.5-inch-wide text column and 9-inch text height**. This is not a two-column format: column width equals full text width. Use ordinary full-width figures, not `figure*`, 0.48-width defaults, or dense graphics rescued by shrinking. Render labels at least 9 points. None of the planned figures needs more than one panel per row.\n\n| Figure | Required design and evidence | Footprint and placement | Readability limits |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `score_prediction_overview` | One conceptual information-flow diagram: historical responses/full source scores → source-only shared subset design and decoder selection → legal target query answers → predicted full score; true retained target score remains beyond an external-scoring boundary. Mark nonlinear decoding optional/source-selected. Use `generate-concept-figure`. | Page 2, full 5.5-inch width; about 2.0-inch drawing height plus short caption, roughly 0.30 body page. Full width is necessary for legible matrix roles and information boundaries. | One panel, at most six blocks/categories, three visual information roles/series, at least 9-point labels. |\n| `paired_error_comparison` | Horizontal forest plot of selected-minus-baseline MAE for uniform, prior, anchors, and IRT using the single supplied aggregate interval per comparator; common zero line. Generate with matplotlib. | Around page 6, full 5.5-inch width; about 1.7-inch axes height, roughly 0.26 body page with caption. Comparator labels, signed ticks, and intervals need the complete span. | One panel, four comparator categories, one series. Do not clip interval endpoints or duplicate the interval by seed. |\n| `case_budget_reversals` | Two vertically stacked horizontal dot-plot panels: five leaderboard cases and four HELM cases. Each case has three offset budget symbols for candidate-minus-local-IRT MAE. Common signed x-axis; no connected progression lines. Generate with matplotlib. | Around page 7, full 5.5-inch width; about 3.2-inch total axes height, roughly 0.43 body page with caption. Stacking avoids illegible adjacent mini-panels and preserves all failure cells. | Two panels, one per row; at most five cases and three budget series per panel; shapes plus color, at least 9-point labels. |\n\nOnly the overview uses the concept-figure workflow; the downstream orchestrator, not this framing stage, owns its execution. Preserve editable source and vector exports when available. Quantitative figures must have reproducible source and **both PDF and PNG exports**, with PNG at 300 DPI. LaTeX references the PDF whenever one exists. For raster-only full-width placement, regenerate at **at least 2400 pixels wide**; any justified narrower/column raster-only placement needs at least 1200 pixels. A 300-DPI preview at 5.5 inches is not enough for the raster-only full-width rule. Never upscale an existing image.\n\n## Body Budget and Scientific Structure\n\nTarget about **8.5 body pages**, excluding references and substantive Supplementary Information:\n\n| Material | Body allocation including its floats |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Title, abstract, introduction, early overview | 1.5 pages |\n| Problem/estimand and focused related work | 1.0 page |\n| Weighted acquisition, decoding, source-only tuning | 1.8 pages |\n| Frozen data, fair protocol, baseline definitions | 1.1 pages |\n| Aggregate uncertainty, ranking, case/budget reversals | 2.0 pages |\n| Interpretation, limitations, conclusion | 1.1 pages |\n\nSuggested headings: **Introduction; Problem and Related Work; Score-Targeted Subset Prediction; Evaluation Protocol; Results** with subsections **Aggregate Accuracy and Conditional Uncertainty** and **Benchmark-Specific Reversals and Ranking; Scope and Limitations; Conclusion**.\n\nSpend available space on precise estimands, the acquisition/decoder equations, group weighting, fold-local refitting, baseline definitions, interpretation of failures, and uncertainty assumptions. Do not add unsupported ablations, inflated related work, repeated plots, or oversized graphics to fill pages. The venue requires 8–9 body pages; an eight-page body must have its final page at least 75% full, and a nine-page body must not overflow the body limit. Check final layout later, not by final-paper validation during framing.\n\n## Supplementary Information After References\n\n**Purpose:** add reproducibility and complete evidence disaggregation without hiding any essential result, reversal, or limitation. Later authoritative appendix rankings can add material here without retroactively changing this strategy.\n\n1. **Weighted Acquisition and Decoder Specification.** Full weighted covariance/conditional-gain and decoder equations, source-only tuning shortlists and group partitions, optional branch, clipping, and zero-budget/insufficient-group fallbacks. Ground in the inspected selected source tree linked to `final.evidence.episodes` and `final.qualification.status`. Clearly distinguish specification from ablation evidence.\n2. **Frozen Data and Lineage/Content Provenance.** Pinned snapshot, rebuild script, retained columns, actual evaluated dimensions, grouping rules, hashes, and conservative ancestry/content limitations. Ground the evaluated data link in `final.evidence.episodes` and detail provenance from the local manifests. Do not substitute generic task-brief model counts.\n3. **Complete Benchmark, Budget, and Seed Results.** Every non-replay normal cell and its four baseline prediction arrays; reproducible MAE/ranking extraction and reconciliation to the recorded final metrics. Newly derived baseline statistics are postprocessing, not new runs; no invented cell intervals. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.mae`, `final.metric.rmse`, `final.metric.spearman`, `final.metric.kendall`, and the four baseline `final.metric.*` entries.\n4. **Paired Uncertainty and Observation Controls.** The single fixed-case bootstrap, checkpoint/seed retention, equal-case weights, evaluator missing-cell policy, recorded zero failed normal cells, and intervention/replay/duplicate-history definitions. A no-failure count is not a performance-success rate or universal validity certificate. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.constant_mae`, `final.metric.permuted_mae`, `final.qualification.outcome`, `final.qualification.status`.\n5. **Reproduction and Evidence Boundaries.** Anonymized reconstruction/evaluation commands based on existing scripts and dependency/specification files, seeds/budgets, hashes, and an eventual script-to-table/figure mapping. Label unrerun commands truthfully, report CPU requirements as requirements, and give no unmeasured speedup or end-to-end time. Include the absence of component ablations/branch-selection diagnostics. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes` and all three `final.qualification.*` entries.\n6. **LLM Usage.** The separately required venue section: precise documented roles in ideation, implementation, citation-query planning, framing, and eventual writing/figure assistance; distinguish evaluator measurements from agent-produced interpretation. Disclose actual verification, not fictitious human review. Framing used local inspection and existing-prediction postprocessing, not candidate execution. Link measurements to `final.evidence.episodes`; process roles come from the supplied task/memory provenance, which is not quantitative evidence.\n\n## Literature and Readiness Boundary\n\nThe supplied bibliography is mostly off-topic. The retrieval log shows its quota was exhausted before the planned task-specific title queries; relevant source metadata does not imply source reading. Some existing foundational entries may be useful leads, such as `Derezinski2019BayesianED`, `Ravi2016ExperimentalDO`, and `Uto2020AGM`, but their exact relevance must be checked and they do not substitute for the permitted benchmark-prediction literature.\n\nBefore final related-work writing, repair this gap using permitted direct-title sources from the task brief and focused unrelated foundational material, maintain citation provenance, and cite only genuinely consulted relevant works with actual BibTeX keys. Do not cite tangential engineering papers to reach a target count. This is a **citation-readiness prerequisite**, not a reason to invent a weaker or broader empirical story. This stage does not modify references or perform retrieval.\n\nNever infer or search for the prohibited paper's identity or expand citation networks toward it. If a later accidental encounter occurs, stop and document location and reading extent. This local-only framing stage identified no such encounter. Omit authors, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, personal links and internal URLs from the anonymous manuscript. Do not invent a venue checklist requirement absent supporting venue instructions.\n\n## Anti-Diary and Overclaim Guardrails\n\nAvoid headings **Search Progression; Campaign Phases; Milestones; First Valid Result; Iteration History; Experiment A / Experiment B; From Audit to Ridge to Nonlinear; Winning Configuration; Nonlinear Decoder Ablation; Universal Benchmark Prediction**.\n\nAvoid wording **we then tried; subsequent improvements; first valid result; next-best; later variants; state of the art; wins on every benchmark; proves generalization; leakage-free; the nonlinear component caused the gain; unbiased rankings; adaptive per-target acquisition**. Internal field names and pipeline labels listed in the JSON forbidden phrases are private bookkeeping, never visible metrics or prose recommendations.\n\nDo not conflate source-only fitting with complete validity; score error with ranking preservation; aggregate confidence with new-benchmark uncertainty; local baseline families with exact published systems; or an implemented optional module with a measured component gain. Anonymization, qualification facts, mixed results, and scientific limitations must survive later writing, review, audit, and revision.\n\n## Stage Boundary\n\nDeliver only `paper_strategy.json` and this mirrored memo, with append-only memory updates. Do not create `paper.tex`, `claims_grounding.json`, figures, new experiments, or final-paper validation outputs. Validate strategy schema, source-ID existence, figure budgets, qualification preservation, and memo agreement only. No final paper has been drafted in this stage.\n{\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-17T14:17:27.991263+00:00\",\n  \"checks_status\": \"passed_local_checks\",\n  \"body_pages\": 8,\n  \"total_pages\": 14,\n  \"references_start_page\": 9,\n  \"supplementary_start_page\": 11,\n  \"local_last_body_page_geometric_fill\": 0.9511368951679747,\n  \"citation_count\": 25,\n  \"claim_count\": 66,\n  \"locator_checks\": 93,\n  \"safe_resolved_tex_input_count\": 116,\n  \"canonical_limitations\": [\n    \"Canonical figure CLI imports unavailable src.orchestrator; canonical figure JSON Schema and declared strategy density limits checked directly.\",\n    \"Canonical compile recorder rejects Debian /usr/share/texmf/web2c/texmf.cnf symlink resolving inside separately approved /usr/share/texlive. Sanitized compile and canonical bibliography/rendered-SI checks run separately; all resolved input paths checked against the union of approved roots.\",\n    \"Rendered page geometry is a local cross-check, not the parent orchestrator authoritative venue report.\",\n    \"Concept overview is an orchestrator-owned placeholder pending synchronous rendering.\"\n  ]\n}\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Append validated local/global memory entries for idea workflows.\n\nTwo modes:\n\n* default (event append) — appends one memory event to ``memory.md`` (local +\n  global) plus one JSONL record to ``memory.jsonl``. Existing behavior.\n* ``--write-conclusions`` — atomically replaces ``conclusions.md`` with a\n  validated single-paragraph distilled summary and appends a record to\n  ``conclusions_history.jsonl``. Validation rejects empty input,\n  multi-paragraph input, or input over 300 words.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport fcntl\nimport json\nimport os\nimport stat\nimport sys\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Any\n\nCONCLUSIONS_WORD_LIMIT = 300\nGATE_TRIGGER_VALUES = (\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\")\n\nLOCAL_MEMORY_HEADERS = \"\"\"# Memory Log\n\n## Context\n## Idea Discoveries\n## Program Evaluations\n## Failures / Regressions\n## Next Hypotheses\n\"\"\"\n\nGLOBAL_MEMORY_HEADER = \"# Global Memory Log\\n\\n\"\n\n\ndef utc_now() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the current UTC timestamp string.\"\"\"\n    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat()\n\n\ndef _load_event(*, event_json: str | None, event_file: Path | None) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Load event.\"\"\"\n    if event_json:\n        parsed = json.loads(event_json)\n    elif event_file is not None:\n        parsed = json.loads(event_file.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n    else:\n        raw = input().strip()\n        if not raw:\n            raise ValueError(\"missing event payload\")\n        parsed = json.loads(raw)\n    if not isinstance(parsed, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"event payload must be a JSON object\")\n    return parsed\n\n\ndef _ensure_required_fields(event: dict[str, Any]) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Ensure required fields.\"\"\"\n    normalized = dict(event)\n    if not isinstance(normalized.get(\"timestamp\"), str) or not normalized[\"timestamp\"].strip():\n        normalized[\"timestamp\"] = utc_now()\n\n    required_string_fields = (\"step\", \"scope\", \"summary\", \"evidence\", \"next_action\")\n    for key in required_string_fields:\n        value = normalized.get(key)\n        if not isinstance(value, str) or not value.strip():\n            raise ValueError(f\"missing required string field: {key}\")\n        normalized[key] = value.strip()\n\n    for key in (\"idea_id\", \"attempt_id\", \"program_id\"):\n        value = normalized.get(key)\n        if value is None:\n            continue\n        if not isinstance(value, str):\n            normalized[key] = str(value)\n        normalized[key] = normalized[key].strip() or None\n\n    metrics = normalized.get(\"metrics\")\n    if metrics is not None and not isinstance(metrics, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"metrics must be an object when present\")\n    rubrics = normalized.get(\"rubrics\")\n    if rubrics is not None and not isinstance(rubrics, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"rubrics must be an array when present\")\n    tags = normalized.get(\"tags\")\n    if tags is not None:\n        if not isinstance(tags, list):\n            raise ValueError(\"tags must be an array when present\")\n        normalized[\"tags\"] = [str(item).strip() for item in tags if str(item).strip()]\n    return normalized\n\n\ndef _markdown_entry(event: dict[str, Any]) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Handle markdown entry logic for this module.\"\"\"\n    lines = [\n        f\"### {event['timestamp']} | step={event['step']} | scope={event['scope']}\",\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Validate figure manifest artifacts and basic figure readability budgets.\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nfrom src.orchestrator.paper_step import read_json, validate_figure_manifest\nfrom src.orchestrator.tex_graphics import read_tex_file_bounded\n\n\ndef parse_args() -> argparse.Namespace:\n    \"\"\"Parse command-line arguments.\"\"\"\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\"Validate figure manifest artifacts.\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--paper-dir\", default=\".\")\n    return parser.parse_args()\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    \"\"\"Run the command-line entry point.\"\"\"\n    args = parse_args()\n    paper_dir = Path(args.paper_dir).resolve()\n    tex_path = paper_dir / \"paper.tex\"\n    paper_data_path = paper_dir / \"paper_data.json\"\n    references_path = paper_dir / \"references_provenance.json\"\n\n    if not tex_path.exists():\n        raise SystemExit(f\"Missing required artifact: {tex_path}\")\n\n    tex_text = read_tex_file_bounded(tex_path)\n    paper_data = read_json(paper_data_path)\n    references = read_json(references_path)\n    grounding_sources = paper_data.get(\"grounding_sources\")\n    if not isinstance(grounding_sources, dict):\n        raise SystemExit(\"paper_data missing grounding_sources object.\")\n    allowed_source_ids = set(str(key) for key in grounding_sources)\n    refs = references.get(\"references\")\n    if isinstance(refs, list):\n        for row in refs:\n            if isinstance(row, dict):\n                source_id = row.get(\"id\")\n                if isinstance(source_id, str) and source_id.strip():\n                    allowed_source_ids.add(source_id)\n\n    report = validate_figure_manifest(\n        paper_dir,\n        tex_text=tex_text,\n        allowed_source_ids=allowed_source_ids,\n    )\n    if not bool(report.get(\"checked\", False)):\n        print(\"No figures referenced or required; figure validation skipped.\")\n        return 0\n\n    hard_errors = report.get(\"hard_errors\", [])\n    if not isinstance(hard_errors, list):\n        raise SystemExit(\"figure_manifest validation returned invalid hard_errors payload\")\n    soft_warnings = report.get(\"soft_warnings\", [])\n    if not isinstance(soft_warnings, list):\n        raise SystemExit(\"figure_manifest validation returned invalid soft_warnings payload\")\n    if hard_errors:\n        raise SystemExit(\"Figure validation failed: \" + \"; \".join(str(error) for error in hard_errors))\n    if soft_warnings:\n        print(\"Figure validation warnings: \" + \"; \".join(str(message) for message in soft_warnings))\n\n    print(\n        f\"Validated {int(report.get('figure_count', 0))} figures with manifest and readability budgets.\"\n    )\n    return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main())\n# Reproducing the manuscript\n\n## Scope\n\nThe paper analyzes the supplied, completed final evaluation. It does not report\na fresh estimator run. `paper_data.json` is immutable. Development scores are\nnot pooled with final results. No new component ablations were executed.\nThe evaluator records final status `completed` and outcome `qualified`; these\nfacts do not certify generalization. Exact identity between the available\nselection source counterpart and the final source-tree digest is not independently\nverified. See `source_evidence/inventory.json`.\n\n## Analysis environment and commands\n\nPython 3.12 is used. Install tools outside this workspace:\n\n```sh\npython3 -m venv /tmp/paper-reproduction\n/tmp/paper-reproduction/bin/pip install -r requirements-paper.txt\n/tmp/paper-reproduction/bin/python scripts/build_paper_artifacts.py\n/tmp/paper-reproduction/bin/python scripts/build_claim_mapping.py\nbash .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir .\nbash .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\n\nLaTeX requires pdfLaTeX, BibTeX, the usual AMS/natbib/graphics/Times packages,\nand `pdftotext`. The official ICLR 2026 style and bibliography style were retrieved\ndirectly from `https://github.com/ICLR/Master-Template/tree/master/iclr2026`.\nThey retain the official single-column 5.5-inch by 9-inch text geometry.\nThe venue-specific brief selects ICLR 2026, even though newer template files\nexist upstream. No author names, affiliations, acknowledgments or funding are added.\n\nThe reconstruction script reads all prediction arrays, recomputes metrics with\nthe evaluator's weighting, and asserts agreement with the recorded final scalar\nmetrics. It excludes the replay record and deduplicates the repeated bootstrap\nobject. No bootstrap is rerun. The CSV contains every retained cell and estimator;\nthe JSON also contains seed/budget/case means. Prior ranking artifacts are retained\nin the numerical reconstruction for audit, but omitted from the paper because\nconstant predictions do not define meaningful rankings.\n\nThese are CPU-only postprocessing operations. No portable runtime estimate or\ninference-speedup measurement is available; wall time depends on environment and\nLaTeX installation. The evaluator itself declares a 10,800-second ceiling, not a\nmeasured expected runtime for reproducing this manuscript. Do not interpret that\nceiling as an estimate or as new timing evidence.\n\n## Artifact-to-script map\n\n- Main aggregate table: `scripts/build_paper_artifacts.py` -> `tables/aggregate.tex`.\n- Complete case/budget table: same script -> `tables/case_budget.tex`.\n- Seed table: same script -> `tables/seeds.tex`.\n- Paired interval figure: same script -> `figures/paired_error_comparison.{pdf,png}`.\n- Case reversal figure: same script -> `figures/case_budget_reversals.{pdf,png}`.\n- Supplemental budget figure: same script -> `figures/budget_sensitivity.{pdf,png}`.\n- Concept overview: orchestrator-owned `generate-concept-figure` workflow,\n  `figures/score_prediction_overview.png`, with its adjacent prompts log. The build\n  script creates an explicitly non-data placeholder only when no render is present;\n  it never replaces an existing figure, nonempty plan log, or completion marker.\n- Claim mappings: `scripts/build_claim_mapping.py` -> `claims_grounding.json`.\n\nQuantitative figures are exported to vector PDF and 300-DPI PNG; the LaTeX uses\nthe PDFs. The full-width conceptual placeholder is at least 2400 pixels wide\nand is reserved for replacement by the orchestrator, not an empirical diagram.\nDerived artifacts have adjacent provenance sidecars.\n\nThe supplementary tables are embedded verbatim in `paper.tex` between named\ngenerated-table markers because the strict supplementary validator disallows\nexternal TeX imports. Their values are generated by the table scripts above;\n`scripts/validate_local.py` verifies byte-equivalent content between the embedded\ntables and generated table files. The main aggregate table is imported normally.\n\nIn this container the canonical figure CLI cannot import the unavailable\n`src.orchestrator` package. The canonical manifest schema and the strategy's\ndensity limits are checked directly instead. The canonical compile recorder\nrejects a standard Debian TeX configuration symlink from `/usr/share/texmf` into\nthe separately approved `/usr/share/texlive` root. `scripts/compile_local.sh`\ntherefore runs the same sanitized pdfLaTeX/BibTeX steps, canonical bibliography\ncheck and rendered-SI check separately. `scripts/validate_local.py` additionally\nchecks every resolved dependency against the union of the approved TeX roots.\nThese environment-specific fallbacks do not alter the supplied validation code\nand do not replace the parent's authoritative venue/layout validation.\nThe local audit writes `paper_checks.json`; Poppler executables must be on PATH.\n\n## Evaluated resources and optional independent rerun\n\nThe supplied task resources contain frozen binary NPZ matrices, content-column\nJSONL, and provenance manifests in `task/resources/final`. A byte-for-byte copy of\neach manifest is in `source_evidence/manifests`. The source data version is\n`socialfoundations/benchmark-prediction` commit\n`55faff496947789f94e6b5908c77cbc3e84f81ad`. Its packed `.jbl` files can be downloaded\nwithout credentials using this exact commit, for example:\n\n```sh\ncurl -L https://raw.githubusercontent.com/socialfoundations/benchmark-prediction/55faff496947789f94e6b5908c77cbc3e84f81ad/data/scores/bbh.jbl -o /tmp/bbh.jbl\n```\n\nThe complete final matrix list is `bbh`, `gpqa`, `mmlu_pro`, `musr`,\n`openllm_math`, `legalbench`, `math`, `med_qa`, and `mmlu`. Source/target lineage\nassignments and cross-plane content removals must match the frozen manifests;\nraw upstream downloads alone are insufficient. The provided\n`task/resources/rebuild.py` describes reconstruction with the mounted column\nmetadata under `/extra/t07-open-llm-leaderboard-columns`. Do not claim reproduction\nif these resources are unavailable or their hashes differ.\n\nEstimator dependencies are copied verbatim to\n`source_evidence/evaluator/requirements.txt`: NumPy 1.26.4, SciPy 1.12.0,\nscikit-learn 1.4.2, joblib 1.4.2 and threadpoolctl 3.5.0. The selected counterpart's\nentrypoint, full source and specification are under `source_evidence/selected`.\nThe final evaluator is standalone in `source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py`.\n\nThe following is an optional fresh evaluation command, **not executed during\nwriting**. It requires the original task resource tree and the evaluator's OS\nisolation support; do not overwrite the sealed results:\n\n```sh\nTASK_SOURCE_DIR=\"$PWD/task\" /path/to/evaluation-venv/bin/python \\\n  source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py \\\n  --program_path \"$PWD/source_evidence/selected\" \\\n  --results_dir /tmp/independent-benchmark-evaluation\n```\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
