{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_49","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"rg -n 'forest_plot|faceted_horizontal_dot_plot|forest|dot.plot|figure_type' paper_strategy.md; sed -n '72,113p' paper_strategy.json; sed -n '1,35p' paper_strategy.md; sed -n '49,59p' scripts/validate_local.py; sed -n '55,88p' .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"82:1. **Aggregate accuracy and paired uncertainty.** A compact five-method table gives absolute MAE, observation cost, and separately labeled RMSE/ranking where supported. The forest plot shows the four supplied paired MAE differences and intervals. No performance-success threshold or internal pipeline metric is needed.\n96:| `paired_error_comparison` | Horizontal forest plot of selected-minus-baseline MAE for uniform, prior, anchors, and IRT using the single supplied aggregate interval per comparator; common zero line. Generate with matplotlib. | Around page 6, full 5.5-inch width; about 1.7-inch axes height, roughly 0.26 body page with caption. Comparator labels, signed ticks, and intervals need the complete span. | One panel, four comparator categories, one series. Do not clip interval endpoints or duplicate the interval by seed. |\n97:| `case_budget_reversals` | Two vertically stacked horizontal dot-plot panels: five leaderboard cases and four HELM cases. Each case has three offset budget symbols for candidate-minus-local-IRT MAE. Common signed x-axis; no connected progression lines. Generate with matplotlib. | Around page 7, full 5.5-inch width; about 3.2-inch total axes height, roughly 0.43 body page with caption. Stacking avoids illegible adjacent mini-panels and preserves all failure cells. | Two panels, one per row; at most five cases and three budget series per panel; shapes plus color, at least 9-point labels. |\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"The inspected paper.sty sets one 5.5-inch-wide column and 9-inch text height. Use the full 5.5-inch text width, about 2.0 inches of drawing height plus a short caption, early on page 2; a half-width figure would make matrix labels and the training/evaluation boundary too small. Allocate about 0.30 body page including caption. Use one panel, at most six labeled blocks, and at least 9-point rendered labels.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"paired_error_comparison\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Show the signed aggregate error difference and conditional uncertainty against every recorded baseline without equating average gains with universal wins.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"forest_plot\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"baseline_method\",\n      \"metric\": \"candidate_minus_baseline_mae_with_recorded_95_percent_interval\",\n      \"split_by\": \"none_equal_case_budget_seed_aggregate\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Use the single-column 5.5-inch text width and about 1.7 inches of axes height, with four comparator rows and one point/interval per row. The full span preserves readable comparator names, signed ticks, and a zero reference at 9-point or larger type. Place near the aggregate results, around page 6; budget about 0.26 body page including caption. Do not squeeze it into a hypothetical two-column slot or truncate interval endpoints.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 4,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"case_budget_reversals\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Expose budget-specific benchmark heterogeneity, including where the selected package loses to the strongest recorded aggregate baseline.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"faceted_horizontal_dot_plot\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"benchmark\",\n      \"metric\": \"candidate_minus_local_irt_mae\",\n      \"split_by\": \"data_ecosystem_with_budget_symbols\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"At the inspected 5.5-inch single-column width, stack the five-case and four-case panels vertically in one column rather than shrinking nine case labels into adjacent mini-panels. Target about 3.2 inches total axes height, one shared signed x-scale, three offset symbols per case for 5/10/25 items, and labels at least 9 points. Place around page 7 and budget about 0.43 body page including caption. Use labels or shapes as well as color; never suppress positive differences or connect points as a search trajectory.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 2,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 5,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 3\n    }\n  ],\n# Paper Framing Strategy\n\n**Status:** ready for a bounded standalone study; relevant-literature repair is required before final related-work writing.  \n**Prompt family:** `paper_standalone` / `write-paper-standalone`.  \n**Provisional title:** *Score-Targeted Subsets for Predicting LLM Benchmark Accuracy*.\n\n## Central Question and Thesis\n\n**Question.** How accurately can a source-trained, score-targeted shared item subset and decoder predict retained full-benchmark accuracy and model rankings at 5-, 10-, and 25-item budgets, relative to local benchmark-estimation baselines, and where does the comparison reverse?\n\n**Thesis.** On nine frozen benchmark matrices, the evaluated acquisition-and-decoding package has lower average accuracy-prediction error than the recorded baselines under equal benchmark, seed, and budget weighting. Its advantage over local IRT is not uniform across benchmarks. The measurements neither isolate an individual decoder component's benefit nor establish transfer beyond this snapshot.\n\nThis is one estimation question with condition-specific evidence from leaderboard and HELM matrices, not two chronological experiments. Lead with the estimand, information constraints, and paired comparison, then explain where the average is misleading. A lower average error is the measured result; universal superiority and nonlinear-mechanism attribution are not.\n\n## Independent Evidence and Qualification Audit\n\nRead the final record independently of any campaign narrative. `downstream_study` is absent. The authoritative qualification sources are:\n\n- `final.qualification.status`: `completed`.\n- `final.qualification.outcome`: `qualified`.\n- `final.qualification.correct`: `true`.\n\nThese facts do not establish complete ancestry separation, contamination absence, out-of-snapshot validity, or any unreported gate diagnosis. Publication readiness is not scientific qualification. If a later authoritative record reports failed qualification, preserve that failure and its reported limitations throughout writing, review, audit, and revision; never infer its cause from retained scores. Missing diagnostics remain unknown.\n\nThe final evidence contains three seeds, nine distinct cases, and budgets 5/10/25. A BBH normal replay is a determinism check, **not** an extra case or an independent measurement. The aggregate paired uncertainty object is repeated in each seed wrapper; use it once, not as three independent bootstrap results. Candidate-owned diagnostics are explicitly unverified.\n\n### Primary Numerical Anchors\n\n| Quantity | Recorded value | Grounding source |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| Selected package MAE | 0.038991605535293065 | `final.metric.mae` |\n| Selected package RMSE | 0.04837892636321264 | `final.metric.rmse` |\n| Local IRT MAE | 0.05911223215296765 | `final.metric.irt_mae` |\n| Uniform sampling MAE | 0.10520541810011626 | `final.metric.uniform_mae` |\n| Source prior MAE | 0.08335016635136305 | `final.metric.prior_mae` |\n    jsonschema.validate(figure_manifest, figure_schema)\n    sidecar_schema = json.loads((ROOT / 'scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json').read_text())\n    for path in ROOT.rglob('*.prov.json'):\n        jsonschema.validate(json.loads(path.read_text()), sidecar_schema)\n    texts = [ROOT / 'paper.tex', *list((ROOT / 'scripts').glob('*.py')), *list((ROOT / 'tables').glob('*.tex'))]\n    checked = 0\n    for path in texts:\n        for reference in re.findall(r'@evidence\\[\\[[^\\]]+\\]\\]', path.read_text()):\n            check_pointer(reference)\n            checked += 1\n    manuscript = (ROOT / 'paper.tex').read_text()\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
